From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 20:45:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] DIAGNOSTIC: soc: qcom: qmi: report unhandled messages

Not for source= beyond the investigation it belongs to.

An LTE attach kills this SoC about ten seconds after the modem finishes
programming its GSI channels. The interval is the same every time --
9.7 s and 9.9 s across two runs measured from the last GSI interrupt --
which is a timeout expiring rather than a race or a rail collapsing.

Something asks and gives up. The obvious candidate is QMI, because
mainline's IPA answers exactly two requests:

	ipa_server_msg_handlers[] = {
		IPA_QMI_INDICATION_REGISTER,
		IPA_QMI_DRIVER_INIT_COMPLETE,
	};

Downstream's service implements a good deal more, including the filter
rule installation a bearer needs. And when the modem asks for something
that is not in that table, qmi_invoke_handler() does this:

	if (!handler->fn)
		return;

It drops the message without a word. So the silence this investigation
kept running into was not the absence of an event, it was a dropped
message with no way to see it.

Say something instead. This sits in the generic QMI layer rather than in
IPA on purpose: it costs nothing when every message is handled, and if
the modem is asking some other service for something it is not getting,
that shows up too.

Ratelimited, because a modem that is not getting an answer tends to ask
again.
---
 drivers/soc/qcom/qmi_interface.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/qmi_interface.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/qmi_interface.c
@@ -403,8 +403,14 @@ static void qmi_invoke_handler(struct qmi_handle *qmi, struct sockaddr_qrtr *sq,
 			break;
 	}
 
-	if (!handler->fn)
+	if (!handler->fn) {
+		pr_warn_ratelimited("qmi: no handler on local port %u for type=%u msg_id=0x%04x txn=%u len=%zu from node %u port %u\n",
+				    qmi->sq.sq_port, hdr->type,
+				    le16_to_cpu(hdr->msg_id),
+				    le16_to_cpu(hdr->txn_id), len,
+				    sq->sq_node, sq->sq_port);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	dest = kzalloc(handler->decoded_size, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!dest)
